[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055933 :tutorial/content "When simplifying numerical expressions, it is generally preferable to avoid having radicals in the denominator. In cases where we wish to remove a radical from a denominator, we must **rationalize the denominator**.\n\nIf the denominator of an expression consists of two terms and includes one or more radicals, we rationalize it by multiplying both the numerator and the denominator by the **conjugate** of the denominator. \n\nTo illustrate, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the conjugate of the denominator $1+\\sqrt{2},$ we *flip the sign* between the two terms. So, the conjugate of the denominator is \n\n$$\n1\\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we start by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $1-\\sqrt{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{1-\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\cdot (1-\\sqrt{2})}{(1+\\sqrt{2})(1-\\sqrt{2})} &  \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{(1+\\sqrt{2})(1-\\sqrt{2})} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)(a-b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nApplying the difference of squares formula with $a=1, b=\\sqrt 2$ to simplify the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{(1+\\sqrt{2})(1-\\sqrt{2})} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{{1}^{2}-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{1-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{-1} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2b34f4d4-8931-5c24-8872-dfa9420a1a87" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13463 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055934 :tutorial/content "To simplify an expression like $-(3+b)$, we need to **distribute** the negative sign across each of the terms inside the parentheses. This can be accomplished by treating the negative sign like multiplication by $(-1)$. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- (3 + b) &=  \\\\\n(-1)(3 + b) &=  \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot 3 + (-1) \\cdot b &=  \\\\\n- 3 + (-b) &=  \\\\\n&= - 3 - b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn these cases, the negative sign in front of the parentheses represents the $-1$. However, to save space, only the negative sign is shown." :tutorial/id #uuid "ced2ba0b-aee5-5031-bf3f-fa447d4aa706" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1357 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055937 :tutorial/content "If a rational expression is expressed in factored form, we can sometimes make the expression even simpler by pulling out factors that are common in both the numerator and denominator.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to simplify the rational expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4(2x-3)}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf we decompose $4$ into $2\\cdot 2,$ we can pull out a factor of $2$ from both the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fraction $\\dfrac{2}{2}$ is the same as $1,$ and anything times $1$ is just itself, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} & = \\\\ 1\\cdot \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} \\\\ 2(2x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** There is a shortcut! We can just \"cancel\" a $2$ from the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2x-3)}{\\cancel{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} & = \\\\ 2(2x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5a1da69d-c5e3-5832-9f42-e75315b6c1a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1369 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055948 :tutorial/content "The **product rule for exponents** states that when we multiply two or more exponents that have the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\na^n\\cdot a^m=a^{n+m}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\na^2\\cdot a^3 = a^{2+3} = a^5.\n$$\n\nTo see why the product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nFor the two factors $a^2$ and $a^3$ we have $a^2=a\\cdot a$ and $a^3= a\\cdot a\\cdot a.$ If we now substitute these two expressions into $a^2\\cdot a^3$, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^2\\cdot a^3&=\\\\\n (a\\cdot a) \\cdot (a\\cdot a\\cdot a)&=\\\\\na\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a&=\\\\\na^5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product rule is just a shortcut for the process above." :tutorial/id #uuid "e5759844-cfcd-53d9-9411-c5a117bf0fc6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1476 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055965 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves of the first few powers of $\\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & =-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the fourth power, we return to the value of $1,$ which can also be written as $\\textrm{i}^0 = 1.$ This means that if we continue to increase the powers, we would cycle through the same four values once again, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3f3a42dd591daf109e474eec99e446706e0d8e46f1252540839cd50d957463.png)\n\nSo, for $\\textrm{i}^5$ through $\\textrm{i}^8,$ the exact same pattern repeats. Remembering that $\\textrm{i}^4 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^5&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^6&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^7&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^8&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1 .\n\t}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $\\textrm{i}^9$ through to $\\textrm{i}^{12},$ the pattern repeats again:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^9&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^{10}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^{11}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^{12}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1 \n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to simplify a higher integer power of $\\textrm{i},$ we *divide the power by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder*.\n\nLet's see this idea in action." :tutorial/id #uuid "be735724-c1e7-5d1c-b396-61d58ee81903" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2762 :tutorial/title "The Cyclic Nature of Powers of i"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055968 :tutorial/content "Multiplying a purely imaginary number by a real number is similar to multiplying two monomials.\n\nFor example, we can multiply the purely imaginary number $3 \\textrm{i}$ by the real number $6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 3\\text{i} & =(6\\cdot 3)\\text{i}=18\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can multiply a complex number by a real number using the distributive property.\n\nFor example, we can multiply the complex number $2-3\\textrm{i}$ by the real number $3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n3(2-3\\textrm{i}) &= 3\\cdot2 + 3\\cdot(-3\\textrm{i}) \\[3pt]\n\t\t&= 6 + 3\\cdot (-3)\\textrm{i} \\[3pt]\n\t\t&= 6 -9\\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e54cae3-6a6c-50bb-a0d7-5ddc1fefaaf2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2789 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055969 :tutorial/content "Recall that logarithms that share a common base $n$ obey the following three laws:\n\n- **The Product Rule**\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) + \\log_n(b) = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\n- **The Quotient Rule**\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\n- **The Power Rule**\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nWe can use these laws together to simplify a logarithmic expression.\n\nFor instance, we can write $3\\log_2(x) + \\dfrac 1 2 \\log_2(y)$ as an expression containing a single term.\n\nFirst, using the power rule, we can write\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n\t3\\log_2(x) + \\dfrac 1 2 \\log_2(y) &= \\log_2\\left(x^3\\right) + \\log_2\\left(y^{1/ 2}\\right)\\\n\t&= \\log_2\\left(x^3\\right) + \\log_2 \\left(\\sqrt{y}\\right)\t.\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\log_2\\left(x^3\\right) + \\log_2 \\left(\\sqrt{y}\\right) = \\log_2 \\left(x^3\\sqrt{y}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log_2(x) + \\dfrac 1 2 \\log_2(y) = \\log_2 \\left(x^3\\sqrt{y}\\right).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "435a21a1-8874-500c-93b0-c742ac5f2a73" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2796 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055972 :tutorial/content "Finally, we can multiply two complex numbers just like multiplying two binomials. Again, we use the fact that $\\mathrm{i}^2=-1.$\n\nSo, to multiply $2 + \\textrm{i}$ and $5 + 3\\textrm{i},$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(2+\\textrm{i})(5+3\\textrm{i}) &= 2 (5+3\\textrm{i})  + \\textrm{i} (5+3\\textrm{i}) \\\n\t\t &= 2\\cdot5 + 2\\cdot(3\\textrm{i}) +\\textrm{i}\\cdot 5+ \\textrm{i} \\cdot (3\\textrm{i}) \\\n\t\t&= 10 + 6\\textrm{i} + 5\\textrm{i} +3\\textrm{i}^2 \\\n\t\t&= 10 + 11\\textrm{i} + 3(-1) \\\n\t\t&= 10 + 11\\textrm{i}  - 3 \\\n\t\t&= 7 + 11\\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c3f115ff-3eaa-551b-b31d-11d45b3c2c0d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2856 :tutorial/title "Multiplying Two Complex Numbers"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055973 :tutorial/content "Multiplying two purely imaginary numbers is similar to multiplying two monomials. But we also need to use the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2=-1.$\n\nFor example, to multiply $2\\textrm{i}$ and $3\\textrm{i},$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}(2\\text{i})(3\\text{i}) & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =6{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =6(-1) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKnowing this, we can also multiply a complex number by a purely imaginary number.\nFor instance, to multiply $2\\textrm{i}$ and $4-3\\textrm{i},$ we use the distributive property and the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2=-1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2\\textrm{i}(4-3\\textrm{i})\n\t\t\t&= 2\\textrm{i}\\cdot 4+2\\textrm{i}\\cdot(-3\\textrm{i}) \\[3pt]\n&=2\\cdot 4\\textrm{i}+2\\cdot(-3)\\textrm{i}^2\\[3pt]\n&= 8\\textrm{i}+(-6)(-1)\\[3pt]\n&= 8\\textrm{i}+6\\[3pt]\n\n&=6+8\\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f770f0f-47fd-5f77-b4f3-e19b7dd9e29e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2857 :tutorial/title "Multiplying by a Purely Imaginary Number"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056007 :tutorial/content "Remember that two terms are called **like terms** if they have the same variable part. \n\nAn expression with different terms can often be simplified by **collecting like terms**.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n2x+3x\n$$\n\nThe terms $2x$ and $3x$ are like terms because they both have a variable part of $x.$\n\nWhenever we have two or more like terms in an expression, we can combine them into a single term. Here, we can combine the like terms $2x$ and $3x$ into a single term by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3x & = \\\\ (2+3)x & = \\\\ 5x & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf438600-1106-5d9f-b7c3-6c7c18d3bb77" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8021 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056008 :tutorial/content "To simplify an expression containing parentheses, we expand the parentheses and then collect like terms. \n\nFor example, let's simplify the following expression: \n\n$$\n3(x + 2) + 4x\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first step is to distribute the $3$ over each of the terms in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t3(x + 2) + 4x &= \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t3\\cdot x + 3\\cdot 2 + 4x &= \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t(3x) + (6) + 4x &= \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t3x +6 + 4x &\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second and final step is to collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t3x + 6 + 4x &= \\\\\n\t\t(3x + 4x) + 6 &= \\\\\n\t\t7x + 6\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4f6f52d9-c069-5009-ae28-6eb58953a5cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8024 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056012 :tutorial/content "To solve an equation like $2x+4 =10$, we need to apply both the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t2x+4{\\color{red}\\:-\\:4} &=10{\\color{red}\\:-\\:4}\\\n               2x+0&=6\\\n\t\t2x &=6\n\t}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{2x}{\\color{red}2} &=\\dfrac{6}{\\color{red}2}\\\n                \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{\\color{red}2}} &= 3 \\\n\t\tx &=3\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo $x=3$ is our solution. Let's plug this back into the original equation, to be sure that it's correct.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & =10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+4 & =10 \\\\ 6+4 & =10 \\\\ 10 & =10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** When solving these types of equations, we always apply the addition principle first, and the multiplication principle second." :tutorial/id #uuid "f8f52cdf-9de7-5b27-addc-4bc35612fa15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8055 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056016 :tutorial/content "To **evaluate** the expression $3+x$ for $x={\\color{blue}2}$, we replace $x$ with $\\color{blue}2$ in the expression and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+x & = \\\\ 3+(2) & = \\\\ 3+2 & = \\\\ 5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f4532e0a-1865-5de3-b251-2774b7d5d45d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8079 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056017 :tutorial/content "The usual order of operations (PEMDAS) applies when evaluating expressions. So when an algebraic expression contains parentheses $(\\ldots)$, we calculate what's inside the parentheses *first*. \n\nFor example, we evaluate $6 - (2x+3)$ at $x=3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-(2x+3) & = \\\\ 6-(2\\cdot 3+3) & = \\\\ 6-(6+3) & = \\\\ 6-9 & = \\\\ -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "34dded6d-159e-5c07-9693-340cd7e2080e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8081 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing Parentheses"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056018 :tutorial/content "If an expression contains more than one variable, we evaluate it using the same procedure, replacing each variable with the appropriate value.\n\nFor example, to find the value of $2b + \\dfrac{1}{2}a$ when $a=-2$ and $b=3,$ we replace $a$ with $-2$ and $b$ with $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2b+\\frac{1}{2}a & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2) & = \\\\ 6-1 & = \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5858aaa3-b757-5794-a965-368222b8122f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8082 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing More Than One Variable"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056023 :tutorial/content "The **distributive law** states that multiplying a group of terms by a number is the same as multiplying each term separately by that same number and adding the products.\n\nFor example, let's take a look at the following expression: \n\n$$\n2(3+4).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can evaluate this expression using the distributive law:\n\n![](images/4c/4ca32844e45f756ce88e4d8e6d73e8b69099d2c517157b51fed9bb1f02a699ea.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3+4) & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4 & = \\\\ 6+8 & = \\\\ 14 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distributive law also applies when the multiplication occurs on the right of the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+4)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ 3\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ 6+8 & = \\\\ 14 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "38c806d3-fe46-5786-81c9-63a1d1bdd9d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8112 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056024 :tutorial/content "The distributive law also works when there is subtraction inside the parentheses. \n\nFor example, we can expand the expression $2(3-4)$ as follows:\n\n![](images/05/05ba4424f566b35af2a279b994c3a8f13564560deaa60f1c7c4616f52d2fb8d6.png)\n\nNotice that we keep a plus ($+$) sign between the two terms. This helps us to avoid mistakes when multiplying with negative numbers.\n\nNow, evaluating our expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3-4) & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-4) & = \\\\ (6)+(-8) & = \\\\ 6-8 & = \\\\ -2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distributive law also applies when the multiplication occurs on the right of the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-4)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ 3\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ (6)+(-8) & = \\\\ 6-8 & = \\\\ -2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ac1d8340-9788-5072-a2ae-b1a73d0df048" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8113 :tutorial/title "Dealing With a Minus Inside the Parentheses"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056026 :tutorial/content "Solving a linear equation involves **isolating** the variable on one side of the equation. \n\nThis can often be accomplished using the **addition principle**. The addition principle tells us that adding (or subtracting) the same number on both sides of an equation does not change its solution.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 5.\n$$\n\nThe variable $x$ is not isolated, because we also have a $+4$ on the left-hand side. \n\nTo isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *subtracting* $4$ from $x.$ This way, the $+4$ and $-4$ will cancel out to $0,$ which will leave $x$ alone. Remember to do this to *both* sides of the equation! \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx + 4 &= 5 \\\n    \t\tx+4\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} &=5\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} \\\n\t\tx+(4-4) &=(5-4) \\\n\t\tx + 0 &= 1 \\\n\t\tx &= 1.\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is our solution. Let's check that our solution is correct by plugging it back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\tx + 4 &= 5 \\\n\t\t1 + 4&= 5 \\\n\t\t5 &=5 \\;{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1eab91ef-23e4-5e41-9ef4-72aa3c0eb594" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8126 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056027 :tutorial/content "**Factoring** is the opposite of distributing. To illustrate, consider the expression $4(a-2b)$. If you consider distributing as going forward,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(a - 2b) &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot a - 4 \\cdot 2b &=  \\\\\n&= 4a - 8b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen factoring would be going in reverse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4a - 8b &=  \\\\\n(4 \\cdot a)-(4 \\cdot 2b) &=  \\\\\n&= 4(a - 2b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, you would say that $\\color{red}4$ was **factored out** of $4a-8b$.\n\nTo factor any expression, we just need to follow these two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(4a,8b) = {\\color{red}{4}}$, so:\n$4a - 8b|=; (4 \\cdot a) - (4 \\cdot 2b)$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$(4 \\cdot a) - (4 \\cdot 2b)|=; 4(a - 2b)$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4cfe29e1-cceb-5e7f-8f84-088b24a378c6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8127 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056028 :tutorial/content "When the terms of an expression are negative, we can also factor out a negative sign. For example, the expression $-x-1$ can be factored into $-(x+1)$.\n\nBy the same token, the expression $-2x-2$ can be factored by first factoring out a negative sign, and then factoring out the greatest common factor of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x - 2 &=  \\\\\n- (2x + 2) &=  \\\\\n&= - 2(x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a13481bf-16af-5b80-bf22-74b727eb2c6c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8128 :tutorial/title "Factoring Out a Negative Sign"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056030 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}x = 8.\n$$\n\nThis equation has a **fractional coefficient** of $\\dfrac{2}{3}.$ The term $\\dfrac{2}{3}x$ means that $x$ is being multiplied by $2$ and divided by $3.$\n\nWe can solve this problem by applying the multiplication principle, twice. First, we multiply both sides by ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{2}{3}x &= 8 \\[5pt]\n\t\t{\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\dfrac{2}{3}x &={\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot 8 \\[5pt]\n\\cancel{\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{\\cancel{3}}x &={\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot 8 \\\n\t\t2x &=24\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by ${\\color{red}{2}}$ to isolate $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x &=24\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{2x}{{\\color{red}{2}}} &=\\dfrac{24}{{\\color{red}{2}}} \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{\\color{red}{2}}} &=\\dfrac{24}{{\\color{red}{2}}} \\\n\t\tx &=12\t\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8576ba10-7bde-590e-9219-705766ef6f6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8150 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056031 :tutorial/content "When solving equations with fractional coefficients, there is a shortcut! We can complete division and multiplication in a single step by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of the fractional coefficient.\n\nFor example, to solve the equation $\\dfrac{2x}{5}= 6,$ we first express the fraction as a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6\n\t}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is $\\dfrac{2}{5}$, and its reciprocal is $\\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right)^{-1} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}.$ Because the product of a fraction and its reciprocal is equal to $1,$ we can multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal to isolate $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}  \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{5} }_{\\large =\\, 1} x &= {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot 6 \\[5pt]   \n\t\t1 x  &= \\dfrac{5\\cdot 6}{2} \\[5pt]   \n\t\tx  &= \\dfrac{30}{2} \\[5pt]   \n\n\t\tx &= 15\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "278b0f64-5aa2-525c-b86d-91239623e85b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8151 :tutorial/title "Using the Reciprocal"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056066 :tutorial/content "Let's take a number, say, $-3.$ We can depict it on a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/de/ded8743284f207bff35eba24617fd4245a69d439ed151d09eed564813f9d72fb.png)\n\nThe *distance* between the number $-3$ and $0$ is known as the **absolute value** of $-3.$ We write it as\n\n$$\n|-3| = {\\color{red}3}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $|\\,2\\,|$, the absolute value of the number $2$, is the distance between $2$ and $0$:\n\n![](images/f4/f44fcd05fb0932083d48c8b11892247d8de8fbc46ccf7186fb12843702181aea.png)\n\nWe write $|\\,2\\,| = {\\color{red}2}.$\n\nSo, the absolute value simply \n\n- *removes the minus sign*, if the number is negative, or\n\n- does nothing, if the number is non-negative.\n\nSometimes, we can be asked to evaluate an expression involving the absolute value function, like $|x +1| + 5$ when $x=-3.$ In this case, we evaluate the expression inside the absolute value before simplifying the rest of the expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1|+5 & = \\\\ |(-3)+1|+5 & = \\\\ |-2|+5 & = \\\\ 2+5 & = \\\\ 7 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3325f9a7-d389-562e-ac1b-4eb26671f84b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8817 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056067 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the two polynomials $6x^2 + x +1$ and $2x^2 - 3x + 6$, and we want to add them. To do that, we put a plus sign between the two, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6x^{2} + x + 1) + (2x^{2} - 3x + 6) &=  \\\\\n6x^{2} + x + 1 + 2x^{2} - 3x + 6 &=  \\\\\n\\underbrace{6x^{2} + 2x^{2}}_{{x}^{2}\\text{ terms}} + \\overbrace{x - 3x}^{x\\text{ terms}} + \\underbrace{1 + 6}_{\\text{constants}} &=  \\\\\n(6 + 2)x^{2} + (1 - 3)x + 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 8x^{2} - 2x + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f6745611-70da-526a-9a6c-143b072c13c2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8873 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056068 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to *subtract* the polynomial $2x^2 + x$ from the polynomial $6x^2 - 3x +1$. To do that, we start by putting a minus sign between the two:\n\n$$\n(6x^2 - 3x +1) - (2x^2 + x)\n$$\n\nHere, we need to remember to distribute the minus sign. Once we've done that, we can collect like terms and then simplify as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6x^{2} - 3x + 1) - (2x^{2} + x) &=  \\\\\n6x^{2} - 3x + 1 - 2x^{2} - x &=  \\\\\n6x^{2} - 2x^{2} - 3x - x + 1 &=  \\\\\n(6 - 2)x^{2} + (-3 - 1)x + 1 &=  \\\\\n&= 4x^{2} - 4x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "90da9a1a-3727-51e9-ad38-7db8b7ed3e20" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8875 :tutorial/title "The Difference of Two Polynomials"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056070 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 8\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term $(-8)$ is *negative*. So, when writing down our factor pairs, one number will always be positive, and the other will always be negative.\n\nSo, first, we write down all factor pairs of $-8{:}$\n\n$$\n(1,-8), (-1, 8), (2,-4), (-2, 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + (-8) &= -7 & \\cdot \\\\ (-1) + 8|= 7 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + (-4)|=-2 & ✓ \\\\ (-2) + 4|= 2 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-2x = 2x-4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 8 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (-4x - 8) &=  \\\\\nx(x + 2) - 4(x + 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3b5a5752-232b-5714-b74a-1e50c4210b83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8894 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Constants"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056076 :tutorial/content "Remember that to factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the constant term that adds to the coefficient of the linear term.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 7x+12\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the constant term $(12)$ is *positive*, while\n- the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the constant is *positive* and the coefficient of the linear term is *negative*, we must first consider the *negative* factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$. These are as follows:\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\qquad (-2,-6),\\qquad (-3,-4)\n$$\n\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their *sum* is negative, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}(-1) + (-12) &= -13 & \\cdot \\\\ (-2) + (-6)|=-8 & \\cdot \\\\ (-3) + (-4)|=-7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-7x = -3x - 4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 7x + 12 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} - 3x) + (-4x + 12) &=  \\\\\n&= x(x - 3) + 4(-x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to get a *common* factor in both terms, we take an additional factor of $-1$ from the second term:\n\n$$\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3)\n$$\n\nNow that we have a common factor of $(x-3)$, we factor the expression fully in the usual way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 3)(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "25f481d5-38d8-5f35-95fc-dd74f97d0394" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9504 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060908 :tutorial/content "To add or subtract vectors given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to add or subtract each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2,5⟩+⟨-3,7⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨2+(-3),5+7⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨-1,12⟩ & ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+(-3) \\\\ 5+7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, to multiply a vector given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to multiply each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot ⟨2,5⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨(-5)\\cdot 2,(-5)\\cdot 5⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨-10,-25⟩ & ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-5)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "25665086-90db-5c8c-b344-69f6f45aac54" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2915 :tutorial/title "Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors in Angle Bracket or Column Vector Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060916 :tutorial/content "To add or subtract vectors given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to add or subtract each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨7, 3, 5⟩ + ⟨- 2, 5, 3⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨7 + (-2), 3 + 5, 5 + 3⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= ⟨5, 8, 8⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}7 + (-2) \\\\ 3 + 5 \\\\ 5 + 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLikewise, to multiply a vector given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to multiply each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot ⟨7, 3, 5⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨3 \\cdot 7, 3 \\cdot 3, 3 \\cdot 5⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= ⟨21, 9, 15⟩ \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 7 \\\\ 3 \\cdot 3 \\\\ 3 \\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}21 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f6feafef-8301-505e-959e-aa8481206ec4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2927 :tutorial/title "Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors in Angle Bracket or Column Vector Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060930 :tutorial/content "If we want to multiply one square matrix by another (which has the same dimensions), we multiply each row in the first matrix by each column in the second. \n\nFor example, suppose we want to multiply two matrices \n$A = \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        1 & 6  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack$\nand\n$B = \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & 0  \\\\\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack.$\n\nThe matrices $A$ and $B$ are both $2 \\times 2$ matrices, and their product will be another $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        1 & 6  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack}_{A}\n\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & 0  \\\\\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack}_{B}\n\n\t&= \n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\ast & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast \n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\t}_{C}\n}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the entries of $C$ (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $c_{11},$ the entry at the intersection of the 1st row and 1st column, we calculate the dot product of the 1st row of $A$ and the 1st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{blue}{1} & \\color{blue}{6}  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{red}{-3} & 0  \\\\\n        \\color{red}{2} & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-3}}) + {\\color{blue}{6}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{12},$ the entry at the intersection of the 1st row and 2nd column, we calculate the dot product of the 1st row of $A$ and the 2nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{blue}{1} & \\color{blue}{6}  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & \\color{red}{0}  \\\\\n        2 & \\color{red}{-5}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{0}} + {\\color{blue}{6}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}})   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{21},$ the entry at the intersection of the 2nd row and 1st column, we calculate the dot product of the 2nd row of $A$ and the 1st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {1} & {6}  \\\\\n        \\color{blue}{4} & \\color{blue}{3}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{red}{-3} & 0  \\\\\n        \\color{red}{2} & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        {\\color{blue}{4}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-3}}) + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{22},$ the entry at the intersection of the 2nd row and 2nd column, we calculate the dot product of the 2nd row of $A$ and the 2nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {1} & {6}  \\\\\n        \\color{blue}{4} & \\color{blue}{3}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & \\color{red}{0}  \\\\\n        2 & \\color{red}{-5}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{0}} + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}})\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & -15\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {1} & {6}  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & 0  \\\\\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & -15\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack.\n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f1be57fa-6725-5805-b38d-493945b33af9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2989 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060953 :tutorial/content "To divide one complex number by another, we set up a fraction and then multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the complex conjugate of the denominator.\n\nFor example, to divide $2 - \\textrm{i}$ by $1 + 3\\textrm{i},$ we start by setting up a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {2 - \\textrm{i}} {1 + 3\\textrm{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is $1 + 3\\textrm{i}$ and its conjugate is $1 - 3\\textrm{i}.$\n\nWe multiply both the numerator and denominator by $1 - 3\\textrm{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {2 - \\textrm{i}} {1 + 3\\textrm{i}} \\cdot \\dfrac {1 - 3\\textrm{i}} {1 - 3\\textrm{i}} \n&= \\dfrac {2 - 6\\textrm{i} -\\textrm{i}  + 3\\textrm{i}^2} {1 - 3\\textrm{i} + 3\\textrm{i} - 9\\textrm{i}^2}  \\[5pt]\n&=\t\t\\dfrac {2 - 7\\textrm{i}  + 3(-1)} {1 - 9(-1)}  \\[5pt]\n&= \t\t\\dfrac {2 - 7\\textrm{i}  - 3} {1 + 9}  \\[5pt]\n&= \t\t\\dfrac {-1 - 7\\textrm{i}} {10} \n\t}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b \\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {-1 - 7\\textrm{i}} {10} = \n\t\t-\\dfrac{1} {10} - \\dfrac {7} {10} \\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a39e145f-e575-5bce-9657-178b5e9ba402" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3735 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060980 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above. \n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm c$\" stands for **centi**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{100}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{cm} = \\dfrac{1}{100}\\,\\textrm{m}.$ \n\n Notice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ to itself $100$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{m}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{1\\,\\text{cm}+1\\,\\text{cm}+⋯+1\\,\\text{cm}}^{100\\text{ times}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m}+\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m}+⋯+\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ 100\\times \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ \\frac{100\\times 1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ \\frac{100}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $100\\,\\textrm{cm}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ as stated in the table.\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mm} = \\dfrac{1}{1,000}\\,\\textrm{m}.$ This means that $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ as shown in the table." :tutorial/id #uuid "4a9204c0-7ef9-5037-b2c7-b8c28f64e8c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7943 :tutorial/title "The Prefix System for Metric Units"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060989 :tutorial/content "To multiply powers of the same base, we simply *add* the exponents. This is called the **product rule for exponents**.\nFor example, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3}).$ Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6\n$$\n\n**Note:** The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4}\\times {3}^{2} & = \\\\ \\underbrace{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{4\\text{ copies}}\\times \\underbrace{3\\times 3}_{2\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ \\underbrace{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{6\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3baed7a9-d08b-5c41-8d2c-77d4f4daf35d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8156 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065340 :tutorial/content "To factor a polynomial expression like $2a^2+6a$, we follow the three steps below:\n\n1. First, we find the greatest common factor (or $\\textrm{GCF}$) of both terms. In this case,\n$\\textrm{GCF}(2a^2,6a) = 2a$.\n2. Then, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\textrm{GCF}$:\n$2a^{2} + 6a|=; (2a \\cdot a) + (2a \\cdot 3)$\n3. Finally, we write the $\\textrm{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n$(2a \\cdot a) + (2a \\cdot 3)|=; 2a(a + 3)$\n\nTherefore, the factored form of $2a^2+6a$ is $2a(a+3)$.\n\nWe can check that we factored correctly by multiplying it out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a(a + 3) &=  \\\\\n2a \\cdot a + 2a \\cdot 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 2a^{2} + 6a \\mid ✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f84c09f1-b24a-50f1-a8c4-24b8d857bca2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1595 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065341 :tutorial/content "A **perfect square trinomial** is a three-term expression that is the result of squaring a binomial.\n\nFor example, the expression\n\n$$\nx^2 + 6x + 9\n$$\n\nis a perfect square trinomial because it is the result of squaring the binomial $(x+3)$, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial, we have to figure out what binomial it is the square of. We can often do this by relating the perfect square trinomial to the **square of a sum**:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}2}  {\\color{blue}a}x + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\n \n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2+6x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Factor the trinomial using the \"square of a sum formula\". In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{3}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nThis method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. We will learn more general methods in the future." :tutorial/id #uuid "c04b9f03-f29f-5feb-8d13-7de9fc378a34" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1596 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065342 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves of the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\na^2-b^2 = (a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to factor the difference of two squared monomials.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to factor the expression\n\n$$\nx^2 - 4\n$$\n\nThis expression is a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 2^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can factor the expression using the difference of squares formula with $a=x$ and $b=2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4 &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - 2^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 2)(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3f50b677-50fc-5584-82de-7ad393d19d72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1599 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065367 :tutorial/content "Fractions that contain multiple terms in the numerator and a single term in the denominator can be split into separate terms.\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {x + 2} {3} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac x 3 + \\dfrac 2 3\n\t}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {3a - 4b + 1} {5} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {3a} 5 - \\dfrac {4b} 5 + \\dfrac 1 5.\n\t}\n$$\n\nOften, we're able to simplify the fractions in the individual terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {10x + 9} {2} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {10x} {2} + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {10} {2} \\cdot x + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\t\n\t\t5 \\cdot x + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\n\t\t5x + \\dfrac 9 2 \n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0a37b6ec-6750-5aea-ae26-ad82d059a0ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3375 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065372 :tutorial/content "To compute the integral of any power function $x^n,$ where $n \\neq -1,$ we can use the **power rule for integration**. The power rule for integration states that\n\n$$\n\\int x^n\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac {x^{n + 1}}{n + 1} + C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is any constant and $n\\neq -1.$ For example, in the case of $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the **antiderivative** of $f'(x) = x^3$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac {x^{4}}{4}+C .$ \n\nWe can check that this is the correct answer by taking the derivative of $f(x)$ and seeing that it is indeed equal to $x^3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{x}^{4})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant $C$ disappeared when we differentiated because the derivative of any constant is zero. We always add $+C$ whenever we find the antiderivative of a function because we know that it will disappear when we differentiate.\n\n**Note:** The power rule for integration is just the power rule for differentiation in reverse!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left( x^{n+1} \\right) = (n+1)x^n \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\displaystyle \\int x^n \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9f08a264-7a79-50c1-afb0-903bb2a256f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3852 :tutorial/title "The Power Rule for Integration"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065375 :tutorial/content "To multiply a binomial and a trinomial, we take each term of the binomial and multiply it by every term in the trinomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nFor example, to multiply $({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4),$ we multiply ${\\color{blue}{x}}$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4),$ and then we add this to the product of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)({x}^{2}+2x+4) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2x+4)+3({x}^{2}+2x+4) & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4x+3{x}^{2}+6x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we group the like terms and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4x+3{x}^{2}+6x+12 & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}+4x+6x+12 & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+10x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "abdf5593-94a7-5f4c-a2ec-ca378d5b0034" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4089 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065421 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to simplify the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe expression above is a fraction, but there are additional fractions in the numerator and denominator.\nSuch expressions are called **fractions of fractions**.\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nThe trick to working with fractions of fractions is to remember that a fraction represents a division. So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) \\boldsymbol{\\div} \\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{\\color{red}{4}}{\\color{blue}{3}}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{2}{3})÷(\\frac{3}{4}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{2}{3})\\times (\\frac{4}{3}) & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\times 4}{3\\times 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{9} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5da17ec4-93cc-5398-bbfc-d50405cd2fad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8182 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065422 :tutorial/content "How do we simplify $\\dfrac{5}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}?$ Notice that, this time, we have a counting number divided by a fraction.\n\nThe trick is to write $5 = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{5}}{\\color{red}{1}}$ and perform the same process as before:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{(\\frac{2}{3})} & = \\\\ 5÷(\\frac{2}{3}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{1})÷(\\frac{2}{3}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{1})\\times (\\frac{3}{2}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\times 3}{1\\times 2} & = \\\\ \\frac{15}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "80cee0a8-050d-53ca-8a81-4b6e37f71cdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8183 :tutorial/title "Dividing a Counting Number by a Fraction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065423 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to simplify a fraction of fractions like $\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)}{4}.$ In this case, we have a fraction divided by a counting number. We have two methods available.\n\nThe long way is to write $4 = \\dfrac 4 1$ and then calculate in the same way as we did earlier:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{5}{3})}{4} & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{3})÷4 & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{3})÷(\\frac{4}{1}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{3})\\times (\\frac{1}{4}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\times 1}{3\\times 4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{12} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA quicker way is to recognize that the result will have the same numerator as the top fraction and that the denominator is just the product of the two denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{5}{3})}{4}=\\frac{5}{3\\times 4}=\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "03013ab2-d0a1-57fa-817f-9dd124de63f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8185 :tutorial/title "Dividing a Fraction by a Counting Number"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065425 :tutorial/content "Subtracting a negative number is the same as adding a positive number. To remember this, just think that whenever you see a minus ($-$) sign followed by a negative ($-$) sign, they combine into a single plus $(+)$ sign, like so:\n![](images/54/54cf3d5630d0f6f43570b1c290019e92d035acaaac13b43ac40b7e3b093f988b.svg)\n\nA minus sign followed by a negative sign can be hard to make out, so it's considered better form to place parentheses around the negative number. For example, $9-(-4)$ is the same thing as $9 + 4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\,\\overbrace{-\\,(-}^{+}\\,4) & = \\\\ 9\\,+\\,4 & = \\\\ 13 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t- 16 -(-5) &= - 16 + 5.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo compute the result of adding $5$ to $-16,$ we start at $-16$ and move $5$ places to the right:\n\n![](images/fa/fa636855c7ec14299ddcb750b21561799f2b76848d52ee492f0a868cd77bccf0.png)\n\n \n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-11$. Therefore, \n\n$$\n-16-(-5)=-16+5=-11.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2d792699-d4e9-5272-8abf-a14fdff5a6c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8280 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065434 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we might be given an equation that contains more than one variable and asked to isolate one of the variables. This means that we need to put the variable we're interested in on one side of the equation (on its own) and everything else on the other.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to isolate $n$ in the equation\n\n$$\n2n - k = q + 5k.\n$$\n\nThe equation has three variables, $n,$ $k,$ and $q.$ However, we will treat the variables $k$ and $q$ as constants because we are solving for $n$ only. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $n\\mathbin{.}$ \n\nFirst, we add $k$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2n - k &= q + 5k \\[5pt]\n\t\t2n - k + k &= q + 5k +k \\[5pt]\n\t\t2n &= q + 6k \\\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2n &= q + 6k \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2n} {2} &= \\dfrac {q+6k} {2} \\\nn &= \\dfrac {q+6k} {2}\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nBecause the equation is now solved for $n$, we say that we've made $n$ **the subject** of the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "0a129a33-3921-5f0a-9ed5-78dad53f6359" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8667 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065440 :tutorial/content "When we want to multiply two binomials, we take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nFor example, to multiply $({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})$ and $(x + 2),$ we multiply ${\\color{blue}{x}}$ and $(x + 2),$ and then we add this to the product of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ and $(x+2)$:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})(x + 2) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t{\\color{blue}{x}}(x + 2) + {\\color{red}{3}}(x + 2) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 2x + 3x + 6 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 5x + 6\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0cea50d1-e4ce-511f-821f-0e5e04b6eca1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8877 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065441 :tutorial/content "When we want to calculate the **square of a binomial**, like\n\n$$\n(a+b)^2,\n$$\n\nthere's a formula that we can use instead of working out the product $(a+b)(a+b)$ manually. The formula is\n\n$$\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.\n$$\n\nIt might be helpful to remember this as *\"the square of the first term, plus the square of the second term, plus twice the product.\"*\n\nSuppose we need to calculate the square $(x+3)^2.$ In this case, we have $a= x$ and $b=3.$ We substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ (x)^{2}+2(x)(3)+(3)^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula gives the correct result by working out the multiplication manually:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(x + 3)^2 &= \\[3pt]\n\t\t(x + 3)(x + 3) &= \\[3pt]\n\t\tx(x + 3) + 3(x + 3) &= \\[3pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 3x + 3x + 9 &= \\[3pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 6x + 9 &\\, {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo we get the same result! But by using the formula, we save a lot of time and effort." :tutorial/id #uuid "0266d886-b5db-53fe-bc51-f6486beb6eef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8878 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065442 :tutorial/content "So far, we've been using the formula for the square of a binomial involving addition:\n\n$$\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nThere is also a similar formula that we can use to compute the square of a binomial involving subtraction. The formula is\n\n$$\n(a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2.\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute the square $(x-3)^2,$ we can substitute $a=x$ and $b=3$ into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & = \\\\ (x)^{2}-2(x)(3)+(3)^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula gives the correct result by working out the multiplication manually:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(x - 3)^2 &= \\\n\t\t(x - 3)(x - 3) &= \\\n\t\tx(x - 3) - 3(x - 3) &= \\\n\t\tx^2 - 3x - 3x + 9 &= \\\n\t\tx^2 - 6x + 9 &\\, {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$\n\nAgain, we get the same result." :tutorial/id #uuid "e42b96f5-ca3b-588b-8931-106b975bd0ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8879 :tutorial/title "Binomials With Subtraction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065449 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we learned how to factor quadratic trinomials whose quadratic coefficient equals one. In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include cases where the quadratic coefficient is a positive integer that's different from one.\n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}x^2+{\\color{purple}{7}}x+{\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the quadratic coefficient equals ${\\color{red}{2}}.$\n\nTo factor this trinomial, we must find two numbers that \n\n- *multiply* to give the *product* of the quadratic coefficient and the constant $({\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6),$ and\n\n- *add* to give the linear coefficient $({\\color{purple}{7}})$.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:**  We start by writing down the factor pairs of $6{:}$\n\nThe factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6), \\qquad (2,3).\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:**  Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{7}})\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+6 & =7 & ✓ \\\\ 2+3 & =5 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:**  Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=x+6x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+7x+3 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x+6x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:**  Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+x+6x+3 & = \\\\ x(2x+1)+3(2x+1) & = \\\\ (2x+1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! \n\nWe can check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+1)(x+3) & = \\\\ 2x\\cdot (x+3)+1\\cdot (x+3) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6x+x+3 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+7x+3 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7e6d3a2-936b-5775-9cf9-10135f0e6486" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9455 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065450 :tutorial/content "To factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the *product* of the quadratic coefficient and the constant that adds to the linear coefficient.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}y^2 {\\color{purple}{\\,-\\,7}}y + {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant, ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6$, is *positive*, while\n\n- the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{-7}})$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $6.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-6), \\quad (-2,-3).\n$$\n\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their sum is *negative*, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we determine which of these factor pairs sums to the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{-7}}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-6) & =-7\\,✓ \\\\ (-2)+(-3) & =-5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-y - 6y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-7y+3 & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-y-6y+3 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}-y)-(6y-3) & = \\\\ y(2y-1)-3(2y-1) & = \\\\ (y-3)(2y-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5ff88dae-40df-5a63-a7bd-0d73317e94a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9506 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069927 :tutorial/content "To simplify a rational expression, we cancel out the largest common factor from the numerator and denominator. To do this, we sometimes need to factor the numerator and denominator before canceling.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to simplify $\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}.$ To do this, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {4(x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\t\t\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {\\cancel{2} \\cdot 2(x - 3)} {\\cancel{2}(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\t\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {2(x - 3)} {5x + 2}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "332f534f-811e-5c03-8393-560134dd4ea8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1370 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069940 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, perfect square trinomials have a leading coefficient that is something other than $1.$ For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4x^2+12x+9\n$$\n\nhas a leading coefficient of $4,$ and it is a perfect square trinomial because it is the square of the binomial $(2x+3)$ as we can see below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+2(2x)(3)+(3)^{2} & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial with a leading coefficient other than $1,$ we use the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a+b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $4x^2+12x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+12x+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(6x)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $2x$ and ${\\color{blue}3}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(2x\\cdot 3)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula. In this case, we have $a=2x$ and $b=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** This method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. But we will learn more general methods in the future." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d43b5e8-672e-5cdd-b7a3-8de29e6552f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1597 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069941 :tutorial/content "How can we factor a trinomial that has a negative leading coefficient, like\n\n$$\n-25y^2 + 20y - 4\\,?\n$$\n\n \nWe can reduce this to a more familiar problem by factoring out $−1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(25y^2 - 20y + 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}-20y+4 & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-20y+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(10y)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(5y\\cdot 2)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y-2)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(25{y}^{2}-20y+4) & = \\\\ -(5y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "757d1827-0402-5d3d-b8da-a4dc88c254bb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1598 :tutorial/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial With a Negative Leading Coefficient"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069943 :tutorial/content "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{ab} &=\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to rewrite the square roots of algebraic expressions.\n\nAs an example, let's suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{32y}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite $32y$ as the product $16 \\cdot 2y,$ which contains a perfect square $(16){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{32y}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{16 \\cdot 2y}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{4^2 \\cdot 2y}&\n\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n        \\sqrt{4^2\\cdot 2y}&=\\[3pt]\n        \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&\n\t}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n        \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&=\\[3pt]\n        |4|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&=\\[3pt]\n\n\t4\\sqrt{2y}&\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "169256a7-1898-59b1-a7a9-f8cb8b713d51" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1606 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069944 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we can simplify a radical expression by factoring the expression inside the radical.\n\nFor instance, let's try to simplify the expression\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2+2a+1}\n$$\n\nWe can begin by factoring the expression under the radical, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{a^{2} + 2a + 1} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{a^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot a + 1} &=  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(a + 1)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2} = \\mid a \\mid\n$$\n\nwe can simplify our expression even further. This gives\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(a+1)^2} = \\mid a+1 \\mid\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "16650ca9-91f7-5754-b7e0-04a46cd873e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1613 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069954 :tutorial/content "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the square of a difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a-b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $9x^2-24x+16$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-24x+16 & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(12x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have $a=3x$ and $b=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** Once again, we note that this method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial." :tutorial/id #uuid "609ce9b5-1b4e-5dfc-9d22-4ec6ea2d640b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1856 :tutorial/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069964 :tutorial/content "When solving an equation, we sometimes require taking the square root of a negative number. In such cases, we will not end up with real solutions, but imaginary solutions.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve the equation $2x^2 +8 = 0$ for $x.$ First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x^2 + 8 &= 0 \\[3pt]\n\t\t2x^2 &= -8 \\[3pt]\n\t\tx^2 &= -4\n}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = \\pm \\sqrt{-4}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pm \\sqrt{-4} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{4\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x = \\pm 2 \\textrm{i}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5649dc90-3e67-5125-9612-6e0618aea772" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2786 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069968 :tutorial/content "A **root** of a polynomial is a value that will make the polynomial equal zero when substituted for the polynomial's variable.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $x^2-9.$ This polynomial has two roots, $x=3$ and $x=-3,$ because the polynomial evaluates to $0$ when either of these values is substituted for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}-9 & = \\\\ (3)^{2}-9 & = \\\\ 9-9 & = \\\\ 0 & \\,✓ \\\\ x=-3\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}-9 & = \\\\ (-3)^{2}-9 & = \\\\ 9-9 & = \\\\ 0 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, a number like $x=1$ is *not* a root of the polynomial because the polynomial does not evaluate to $0$ when this value is substituted for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}-9 & = \\\\ (1)^{2}-9 & = \\\\ 1-9 & = \\\\ -8 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5b765b45-ae0c-5c2f-958b-e69d41100abc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3321 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069973 :tutorial/content "To divide one rational expression by another, we multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. Just like with fractions!\n\nFor example, let's divide $x$ by $\\dfrac{3}{y}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx \\div \\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. The divisor is $\\dfrac{3}{y},$ and to get the reciprocal, we just flip the fraction upside down: $\\dfrac{y}{3}.$ Multiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x÷\\frac{3}{y} & = \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{xy}{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!" :tutorial/id #uuid "67b283bf-2859-595a-bacb-ebf32b5f7275" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3379 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069990 :tutorial/content "Remember that in order to add fractions with different denominators, like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4 + \\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\nwe need to put the fractions over a **common denominator**, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt works the same in algebraic rational expressions! To add two fractions, we need to put each fraction over a common denominator. Then, we add the numerators." :tutorial/id #uuid "b8672148-b6a6-587f-bf3c-8b46cf7edae1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5915 :tutorial/title "Adding Rational Expressions Without Common Denominators"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069998 :tutorial/content "To simplify radical expressions, we can use the quotient rule for radicals, which states \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\sqrt {a}} {\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac a b}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}$\n\nFirst, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}  = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{16w^2}} {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{25}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression under the radical as a perfect square and taking its square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{16{w}^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(4w)^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4|\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|w|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7bfc07d9-feb0-5bcc-b3f0-50bb42eb2b6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6691 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070007 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta x$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nFirst, note that the domain of $f(x)$ has been *partitioned* as \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n [1,4] &= [1, 2] \\cup [2, 3.5] \\cup [3.5, 4] .\n}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table. \n\n![](images/2e/2e8563480f46ff335042c3c52a6f60983f871dccb886c8cf3ca0844504a73db6.png)\n\n  \n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our left Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the left endpoint of each interval. \n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n![](images/8f/8f62020b1888f7479665b04028acb822fdccfa9049a37e306ea30ce0aa703ac7.svg)\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\mathcal{A}\t&\\approx\t{\\color{red}1} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2-1})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}4} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}3.5-2})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-3.5}) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= {\\color{red}1} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} +{\\color{red}4} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5}+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5}  \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&=1+6+3\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= 10.\n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86c2c7f1-e8cd-59f5-a5ed-1be84ec0a424" :tutorial/math-academy-id 730 :tutorial/title "Left Riemann Sums with Irregular Step Size"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070018 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to solve the compound inequality \n\n$$\n4 \\le x + 2 \\le 8.\n$$\n\nTo solve the compound inequality, we apply the usual operations to the middle of the inequality to isolate $x.$ Keep in mind that whatever we do to the middle, we also need to do to *both* the left-hand *and* right-hand sides.\n\nHere, we can isolate $x$ by subtracting $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t4 & \\le x + 2 \\le 8 \\[5pt]\n\t4 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} &\\le x + 2 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} \\le 8 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} \\[5pt]\n\t2 &\\le x \\le 6\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $2 \\le x \\le 6.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6d8fae47-5e67-5793-a585-d3781d5c4555" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8677 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070029 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the following equation: \n\n$$\n5^x = 25\n$$\n\nWe can't solve this equation straight off the bat because the two sides of the equation don't share a common base.\n\nHowever, notice that $25$ can be written as a power of $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n25=5^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\n5^x = 5^2.\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation are now written as an exponential expression with a common base of $5,$ we can set the exponents equal to each other and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check that the solution is correct, we plug $x=2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t5^{2} &= 25 \\\n\t\t25 &= 25 \\; {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "43789602-55db-50d6-8517-6be1716d6133" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8839 :tutorial/title "Changing the Base of an Expression"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070037 :tutorial/content "To solve a two-term quadratic equation like $x^2 - 7x = 0$ where there is no constant term, we can factor out the variable and apply the zero-product rule.\n\nFactoring out the variable, we have:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tx^2 - 7x &= 0 \\\n\tx(x - 7) &= 0\n}\n$$\n\nWe apply the zero product rule to get \n\n$$\nx=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x-7=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives \n\n$$\nx=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=0,7.$\n\nTo check that these solutions are correct, we can substitute them back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =0 & \\, & \\, & x & =7 \\\\  & ⇓ &  &  &  & ⇓ \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x & =0 &  &  & {x}^{2}-7x & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}-7(0) & =0 &  &  & {7}^{2}-7(7) & =0 \\\\ 0-0 & =0 &  &  & 49-49 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓ &  &  & 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Warning:** It's tempting to solve the equation by dividing by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-7x}{x} & =\\frac{0}{x} \\\\ x-7 & =0 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we do that, we lose the solution $x=0.$ So, when solving equations, *never divide by a variable* because the variable could be zero, and division by zero is not allowed. Instead, try to factor the variable in the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "bda6c872-00e7-50b0-b4bb-685694b60283" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9238 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074948 :tutorial/content "When dealing with sums of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same number under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 5 + 4\\sqrt 5\n$$\n\nRemember that $2\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\sqrt 5$ mean $2\\cdot\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\cdot\\sqrt 5,$ respectively. \n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}+4\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ (2+4)\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{7}-1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ (2-1)\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 3$ and $\\sqrt 5.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9aa49408-30d5-57b5-8dff-ec1744f9fc6e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1609 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074950 :tutorial/content "We use the **standard algorithm** to divide a two-digit number by a one-digit number. To illustrate, let's divide $92$ by $4$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation, as follows:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens. How many times does $4$ go into ${\\color{red}9}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}2}$ times, and\n\n$$\n4\\times {\\color{blue}2} =8\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}2}$ above the tens, the $8$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $1$. Then we bring the ones digit ($2$) down next to the remainder.\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}9} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}2}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones. How many times does $4$ go into ${\\color{red}12}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}3}$ times, and\n\n$$\n4\\times {\\color{blue}3} =12\n$$\n\nso there is no remainder (\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}12} - 12 = 0\n$$\n\n).\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}3}$ above the ones, the $12$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the final remainder of $0$.\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}3$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}3}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $92$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n92 \\div 4 = 23\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ee791847-fab3-56fb-889d-2d02d7dea9e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1861 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074951 :tutorial/content "If there is a remainder after using the standard algorithm, we write the remainder in the answer. To illustrate, let's find the remainder of $95\\div 8\\,$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $8$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$8$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}9} \\div 8 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $8 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}1$$8$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{7}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}15} \\div 8 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,7$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $8 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}15} - 8 = \\fbox{7}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $95$, so the division is done. However, we still have a **remainder** of $\\color{blue}7$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n95 \\div 8 = {\\color{red}11}\\,\\text{R}\\,{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nHere, $\\color{red}11$ is called the **quotient**." :tutorial/id #uuid "da7b00a0-5a9a-5e5a-acc7-d625c9e3dd99" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1862 :tutorial/title "Dividing Two-Digit Numbers by One-Digit Numbers Using the Standard Algorithm With Remainders"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074952 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm to divide a three-digit number by a one-digit number. To illustrate, let's divide $468$ by $2$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}4}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}2}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}2} =4\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}2}$ above the hundreds, the $4$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $0$. Then we bring the tens digit $(6)$ down next to the remainder.\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 2 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}6}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}3}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}3} =6\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} - 6 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}3}$ above the tens, the $6$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $0$. Then, we bring the ones digit $(8)$ down next to the remainder.\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}3$$2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 2 = {\\color{blue}3}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = 0$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}8}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}4}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}4} =8\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}8} - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}4}$ above the ones, the $8$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the final remainder of $0$.\n\n| $2$$3$$\\color{blue}4$$2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 2 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 8 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $468$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n468 \\div 2 = 234\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ffc2d3f-9be9-55c6-b804-69800505e521" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1866 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074953 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm to find the value of $305 \\div 5$. However, because the hundreds digit ($3$) is less than the divisor ($5$), the process will be slightly different.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\n**Warning**: Notice that $\\color{red}3$ (the number of hundreds in ${\\color{red}3}05$) is less than the divisor $5$, so it doesn't divide. To counter this, we should consider both the hundreds *and* tens. That is, we should divide $\\color{red}30$ by $5$, as follows:\n\n| $\\color{blue}6$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}3} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}30} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}6}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 30$Subtract: ${\\color{red}30} - 30 = 0$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $6$$\\color{blue}1$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$0$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}5} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 5$Subtract: ${\\color{red}5} - 5 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $305$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n305 \\div 5 = 61\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d212998b-5384-5c9a-84f9-9f5780e1b8d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1867 :tutorial/title "Dividing When the Hundreds Digit Is Less Than the Divisor"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074954 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm to find the value of $408 \\div 4$. We will see that the situation here is slightly different. Let's work through it.\n\nAs usual, we start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\color{red}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}0} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}0}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWait! Notice that $\\color{red}0$ is less than the divisor $4$, but not all numbers of the dividend have yet been used! In this case, we need to bring down one more digit:\n\n| $1$$0$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$0$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}2$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$0$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 8 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $408$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n408 \\div 4 = 102\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e2e0d2b-4bb1-5f52-8762-c80e16d26efe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1868 :tutorial/title "Cases Where We Bring Down More Than One Digit at a Time"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074955 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide a four-digit number by a one-digit number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use the algorithm to divide $4,236$ by $3$. We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the thousands:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over thousands: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 3$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 3 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}4$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}3$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = 0$Bring down: $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$4$$\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}3} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 3$Subtract: ${\\color{red}3} - 3 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$4$$1$$\\color{blue}2$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$\\color{red}6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $4,236$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4,236 \\div 3 = 1,412\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7c4b83a1-416a-515e-859c-75235b210520" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1869 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074963 :tutorial/content "When using long division to divide whole numbers, it's often appropriate to round the final answer.\n\nFor instance, let's find the value of $58 \\div 7$, rounded to the nearest tenth. We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{black}8} \\: \\ldots$$7$$\\phantom{\\mid}\\!\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$4$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $1$ decimal place (the nearest tenth), we stop after finding the quotient to $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n58 \\div 7 = 8.28 \\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth. The hundredths place is $8$, which is greater than $5$, so we round up:\n$8.28 \\ldots \\rightarrow 8.3$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f74891d-a04f-506e-a1d4-72f98dfc5324" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1962 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074964 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by whole numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $7.2 \\div 3$.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\,\\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.} 2{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\,2 {\\,\\,. \\,} 4$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\,. \\,} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7.2 \\div 3 = 2.4 \\,\n$$\n\n**Note:** The \"standard algorithm\" is the same as \"long division.\" The two phrases can be used interchangeably." :tutorial/id #uuid "2d1369fc-bc3a-5c6e-b61f-4eca77f96da0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1963 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074965 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by other decimals. The only difference is that we first need to find an equivalent division problem where the divisor is a whole number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $1.11\\div 0.3 \\,$. First, we write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11}{0.3}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11 \\times 10}{0.3\\times 10} = \\dfrac{11.1}{3}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $11.1 \\div 3$. We can compute this using long division as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 7$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\,\\,. \\,} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$2$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$1$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n11.1 \\div 3 = 3.7\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n1.11 \\div 0.3 = 3.7 \\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "53648922-c8dc-502d-af90-e2fcd1b0808f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1964 :tutorial/title "Dividing Decimals Using the Standard Algorithm"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075013 :tutorial/content "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]2 - 6\\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt[3]{2}-6\\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ (4-6)\\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ -2\\sqrt[3]{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[3] 2 + \\sqrt[3] 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3] 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 5.$\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms -- not even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "32078f96-6eaf-57d2-bc71-8af8495d5d6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5996 :tutorial/title "Simplifying Expressions With Cube Roots"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079623 :tutorial/content "To **evaluate** a polynomial at a specific value of $x$, we substitute the value of $x$ into the polynomial and simplify.\n\nFor example, to evaluate the polynomial $2x^2+3$ at $x=5$, we substitute $x=5$ into the expression and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(5)^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(25) + 3 &=  \\\\\n50 + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 53\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d8de8488-40d3-593a-a8a1-9193eb94f8e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1580 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Polynomials"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079624 :tutorial/content "When simplifying expressions, we prefer not to leave a radical in the denominator. To eliminate a radical in an expression's denominator, we can use a process known as **rationalizing the denominator**.\n\nTo rationalize the denominator, we need to multiply both the numerator and denominator by the radical that we wish to remove from the denominator.\n\nFor instance, to rationalize the denominator in the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nall we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{x}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the final expression no longer has a radical in the denominator. Thus, the denominator has been rationalized.\n\n**Note:** Because we multiplied the numerator and denominator by the same amount, the resulting expression $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}}{x}$ is equivalent to the original fraction $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ They are equivalent, even though they are written differently." :tutorial/id #uuid "17e8e5bd-0b03-5315-8d7a-fc97948fb0d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1614 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079636 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves of the names of the sides of a right triangle relative to an angle $\\theta.$ \n\n![](images/4c/4cbe48f70bc4bf67600269883c1260cec2181cf01b50a9675c3177b72d7f954b.png)\n\nThe three fundamental trigonometric ratios, **sine**, **cosine**, and **tangent**, represent the ratios of the sides of a right triangle. The ratios are often shortened to $\\sin$, $\\cos,$ and $\\tan,$ and they are defined as\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin(\\theta) &= \\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{hypotenuse}},  \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\cos(\\theta) &= \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{hypotenuse}},  \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\tan(\\theta) &= \\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{adjacent}}.  \\\n\t}\n$$\n\nFor a given angle $\\theta$, these ratios remain the same no matter what the size of the triangle is. For example, if we were to double the length of each side of a given triangle, the values of these ratios would remain unchanged. \n\nAn easy way of remembering how to compute the different trigonometric ratios is by using the three-syllable acronym **SOH-CAH-TOA**. It stands for:\n\n- SOH $\\rightarrow$ **S**ine = **O**pposite / **H**ypotenuse,\n\n- CAH $\\rightarrow$ **C**osine = **A**djacent / **H**ypotenuse,\n\n- TOA $\\rightarrow$ **T**angent = **O**pposite / **A**djacent." :tutorial/id #uuid "7210f33b-b202-57e9-a4c7-8eb1859c0b97" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2445 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079641 :tutorial/content "The inverse trigonometric functions are particularly useful when we need to find the measure of an angle in a right triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the measure of the angle $\\theta$ indicated in the triangle below.\n\n \n\n![](images/85/85c384f5cbfc9d9bfa4ffebeafc3dbe66eb756e71f4790b0d9566ebdab559f9c.png)\n\nSince we are given the length of the hypotenuse and opposite sides to $\\theta,$ we can use sine to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin (\\theta) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}  &= \\dfrac{5}{7}.\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $\\theta$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin (\\theta)   &= \\dfrac{5}{7}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\arcsin( \\sin (\\theta)) &= \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7} \\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\theta &= \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7} \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=(45.584\\,6...)^\\circ \\[5pt]\n\t\t&\\approx 46^\\circ\n\t}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a55899c-f08a-5577-a1de-9fb483947ddf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2451 :tutorial/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle in a Right Triangle Using Trigonometry"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079643 :tutorial/content "The reciprocals of sine, cosine, and tangent are given special names:\n\n- The reciprocal of **sine** is **cosecant**, abbreviated $\\csc.$\n\n$$\n\\csc(\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\sin(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{opposite}}\n$$\n\n- The reciprocal of **cosine** is **secant**, abbreviated $\\sec.$\n\n$$\n\\sec (\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\cos(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{adjacent}}\n$$\n\n- The reciprocal of **tangent** is **cotangent**, abbreviated $\\cot.$\n\n$$\n\\cot (\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\tan(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{adjacent}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{opposite}}\n$$\n\nLet's write down expressions for $\\csc(B),$ $\\sec(B),$ and $\\cot(B)$ for the triangle below.\n\n![](images/e9/e98dcc332739ff381e8dc43160a907e471062c5b8a75567ba7dbb8687c008efa.png)\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\csc(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac c b \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\sec(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{adjacent}} = \\dfrac c a \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\cot(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac a b \\\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4c546565-505e-5036-a606-7fec250d5073" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2453 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079646 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 1} \\frac{\\sqrt{x} - 1}{x - 1}\n$$\n\nIf we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting $x=1,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator of the function by the **conjugate** of the numerator.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of $\\sqrt{x}-1,$ we simply switch the sign between the two terms to get $\\sqrt{x}+ 1.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\require{cancel}\n\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\frac{\\sqrt{x} - 1} {x - 1}\n\t&= \\lim_{x\\to 1} \\frac{\\sqrt{x} - 1} {x - 1} \\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x} + 1}{\\sqrt{x} + 1}\t\\\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{(\\sqrt{x} - 1)(\\sqrt{x} +1)} {(x-1)(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\\\n        &=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)^2 - 1^2} {(x-1)(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{x-1} {(x-1)(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{\\cancel{x-1}} {\\cancel{(x-1)}(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac1{\\sqrt{x} + 1}\\\\\n        &=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1} +1}\\\\\n\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "73481739-fdce-5a28-84e1-5145ea62af87" :tutorial/math-academy-id 250 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079665 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe first thing to realize is that there is an invisible multiplication symbol here. The above expression is short for\n\n$$\n-3\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\\,\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhen multiplying a radical by a number, we usually omit the additional multiplication symbol.\n\nNow, to simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS.  In terms of the order of operations:\n\n- Expressions underneath radicals are treated as if they are contained within parentheses (the **P** in **P**EMDAS), so we always deal with this first.\n\n- Radicals themselves are treated as exponents (the **E** in P**E**MDAS).\n\nTherefore, following PEMDAS, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{({2}^{4}-1)÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{(16-1)÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{15÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! \n\nTo get a numerical approximation, we need to use a calculator. In this case, we get\n\n$$\n-3 \\sqrt{5} = -3\\times \\sqrt{5} \\approx -6.708\\,204\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." :tutorial/id #uuid "5eb86849-2816-5e44-b2e4-786b170cf6d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3274 :tutorial/title "Expressions Resulting in Radicals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079667 :tutorial/content "Suppose we need to evaluate an expression like\n\n$$\n1+\\sqrt{4},\n$$\n\nwhere we have a square root sign. What would be the order of operations? It turns out that the root signs ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$) can be thought of as parentheses. \nIn other words, we find the value under the square root first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\sqrt{4} & = \\\\ 1+2 & = \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly, let's evaluate $\\big(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27}\\big) \\div 2$.\n\nTo evaluate expressions with radicals, we use PEMDAS as before, but with the following additional rules:\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nFollowing the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27})÷2 & = \\\\ (1-\\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}})÷2 & = \\\\ (1-(-3))÷2 & = \\\\ 4÷2 & = \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3d4a0de2-cecd-5c77-8bf2-40997a53ac9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3292 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079669 :tutorial/content "In more advanced math, we often study shapes that are changing. In these situations, we might be interested to know how one quantity varies with another as a particular shape changes.\n\nFor example, suppose that the area of a rectangle is increasing and that the base's length is twice that of the height. Can we find the instantaneous rate of change of the area of the rectangle with respect to its height?\n\n![](images/3f/3ff74bdb0b346739a978ea87dba07faccedb1e6de6f0534ec2ce27a319c3e668.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of a rectangle is given by  \n\n$$\nA= b h,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $b$ is the base, and $h$ is the height of the rectangle.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the area of the rectangle with respect to its height is given by $A'(h).$ As it stands, we cannot find $A'(h),$ because $A$ is expressed as a function of two variables, $b$, and $h.$\n\nHowever, we are given that $b=2h,$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the height $h$ only by substituting $b=2h$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA= (2h)h= 2h^2.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the area $A$ expressed as a function of $h$ only, we can differentiate. Therefore, the rate of change of $A(h)$  with respect to $h$ is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\nA'(h) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d h}\\left( 2 h^2\\right)\\[5pt]\n        &= 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d h}\\left(h^2\\right)\\[5pt]\n        &  = 2(2 h)\\[5pt]\n        &= 4 h.}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca145476-809e-5b61-99dd-0eafc82900e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3903 :tutorial/title "Rates of Change in a Geometrical Setting"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079717 :tutorial/content "Whenever we have a fraction in an equation, we apply the multiplication principle to get rid of it. For example, consider the equation below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+12}{3} =6\n$$\n\nSince the opposite of dividing by $3$ is multiplying by $3$, we apply the multiplication principle and multiply both sides of the equation by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{2x+12}{3} &=6\\\n\t\t3 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{2x+12}{3}\\right) &= 3 \\cdot 6\\\t\n\t\t\\cancel{3} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{2x+12}{\\cancel{3}}\\right) &= 18 \\\t\n\t\t2x+12 &= 18\n\t}\n$$\n\nThe process of removing the fraction is called **clearing the rational expression**. We now have an equation that we can solve using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x+12 &= 18\\\n\t\t2x+12-12 &=18-12 \\   \n\t\t2x &=6 \\   \n\t\t\\dfrac{2x}{2} &=\\dfrac{6}{2} \\\n\t\tx &=3\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "addac451-9882-5c9f-8aa9-adc3fbd177e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8050 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084412 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the isosceles right triangle whose legs are both equal to $1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d1e8bd928fe666859f7101be65819af5f16fe81df3900941650d279eecd496.png)\n\nBy considering either of the $45^\\circ$ angles in this triangle, we find that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sin 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\[5pt]\n\t\\cos 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{adj.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\[5pt]\n\t\\tan 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{adj.}} = \\dfrac{1}{1} = 1. \n}\n$$\n\nIsosceles right triangles are very common, so the values above occur a lot in trigonometry. It's a good idea to remember them." :tutorial/id #uuid "43cdd368-7207-5d18-8f22-5e384ebbc118" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2457 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084444 :tutorial/content "The **factorial** of a natural number is the product of the number and all of the natural numbers below it. The factorial of a number is denoted by placing an exclamation point after the number.\n\nFor example, the factorial of $4$ is written as $4!$ (with an exclamation point after the $4$), and is computed as\n\n$$\n4! = 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24.\n$$\n\nSo, we say that \"four factorial is twenty-four\".\n\nThe factorials of the natural numbers $1$ through $5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t1! &= 1 \\[5pt]\n\t\t2! &= 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t3! &= 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6 \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t4! &= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24 \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t5! &= 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120\t\n\t}\n$$\n\nNote that the factorial of $0$ is defined to be equal to $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n0! = 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a9766f96-1407-57c0-ad92-13325cf88b1a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3678 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084445 :tutorial/content "One way to simplify a rational expression containing factorials is to calculate the numerator and denominator separately, and then divide the results. \n\nFor example, computing $\\dfrac{10!}{8!}$ using this method, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} {8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = \\dfrac {3\\,628\\,800} {40\\,320} = 90.\n\t}\n$$\n\nHowever, the method above involves lots of computations. Thankfully, there's an easier way! \n\nBefore we multiply, we can cancel out any numbers that appear in both the numerator and denominator, thereby reducing the amount of computation that's needed. Using this method, we get the same result with far fewer computations:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot  \\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1} } { \\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1} } = \\dfrac{10 \\cdot 9}{1} = 90\n\t}\n$$\n\nEven quicker, we could just realize that $10! = 10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8!$ and then cancel the $8!$ that appears in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\dfrac {10!}{8!} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cancel{8!}} {\\cancel{8!}} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9} {1} = 90\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cfc6a557-606a-5683-a8b9-e748e0e86d7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3679 :tutorial/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions that Contain Factorials"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084517 :tutorial/content "If there is a negative sign in a rational equation, we can still solve it using cross-multiplication. The only difference is that we have to move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction first.\n\nFor example, let's solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = -\\dfrac 9 8 .\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the equation from the previous example, but this time, there is a negative sign on the right-hand side.\n\nWe deal with the negative sign by moving it onto the numerator of the fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} .\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed to solve the equation by cross-multiplication, as usual. (We also note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.) Cross-multiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{3}{2x} &= \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} \\[5pt]\n\t\t3 \\cdot 8 &= 2x \\cdot (-9) \\[5pt]\n\t\t24 &= -18x\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t24 &= -18x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{24}{-18} &= \\dfrac{-18x}{-18} \\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{4}{3} &= x\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $x = -\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d61fcf8b-f639-5c4c-8997-66813230bedf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8780 :tutorial/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Negative Signs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101969 :tutorial/content "To raise a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. This is the **power rule for exponents**. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left( 3^5\\right)^4$ by applying the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\left( 3^5\\right)^4 = 3^{5\\cdot 4} = 3^{20}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power rule for exponents is just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({3}^{5})}^{4} & = \\\\ \\underbrace{{3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{5+5+5+5} & = \\\\ {3}^{5\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ {3}^{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "77fdb9f8-30b1-5d1a-bc9d-feb500358913" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3270 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102012 :tutorial/content "Let's see a trick that can be used to compute the sum of the following series:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n (n + 1)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we can use partial fractions to write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n(n+1)} = \\dfrac{1}{n} - \\dfrac{1}{n+1}.\n$$\n\nThen the sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n (n + 1)} = \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} - \\dfrac{1}{n+1}\\right).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, *without* simplifying, notice that many terms cancel:\n\n$\\require{cancel}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =(1\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {s}_{2} & =(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ {s}_{3} & =(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ {s}_{4} & =(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. We say that this infinite series is a **telescoping series**, meaning that it consists of only a finite number of terms after canceling. \n\nSo, if $s_N$ is the $N$th partial sum of the series, we have\n\n$$\ns_N \t= {\\color{blue}{1}} {\\color{red}{\\,-\\, \\dfrac 1 {N + 1}}} .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by computing the limit of $s_N$ as $N\\to\\infty.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{N+1}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7785a1f9-f6fd-5dac-bbab-9da2b149e341" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6768 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102023 :tutorial/content "To divide powers of the same base, we simply *subtract* the exponents. This is known as the **quotient rule for exponents**. For instance, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by subtracting the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{5-2} = {\\color{blue}3}^3.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to the following process:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{5}÷{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {3}^{3+2}÷{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\times \\underbrace{{3}^{2}÷{3}^{2}}_{=1} & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\times 1 & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4547d33f-6904-539f-b917-599af56aac3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8158 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107037 :tutorial/content "The **power of product rule** states that if we want to raise the product $xy$ to some power $n$, we must raise each factor to the power $n$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n(xy)^n=x^n y^n\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n(xy)^4=x^4y^4.\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way, changing the order of factors in the multiplication. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(xy)^{4} & = \\\\ (xy)(xy)(xy)(xy) & = \\\\ (x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x)(y\\cdot y\\cdot y\\cdot y) & = \\\\ {x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of product rule is just a shortcut for the process above." :tutorial/id #uuid "7aabbc0f-a3ca-5f05-b6ae-d7551b16ae1a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1482 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107152 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta{x}$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nThe first thing we note is that the domain of the function $y=f(x)$ is *partitioned* as \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n [1,4] &= [1, 1.5] \\cup [1.5, 2.5] \\cup [2.5, 4].\n}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/60/60c0bfba52fe087b4d8aaeceee367798e24079dc385afff3e83bca60405d4d2b.png)\n\n  \n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our right Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the right endpoint of each interval.\n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n![](images/14/142608196dc9a4498e79c7b5b15850401489b08e0ba658316d14ccf05b47728a.svg)\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\mathcal{A}\t&\\approx\t{\\color{red}2} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}1.5-1})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2.5-1.5})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}8} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-2.5}) \\ \n\t\t\t&= {\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5} +{\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1}+ {\\color{red}8} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5}  \\\n\t\t\t&=1+6+12\\\n\t\t\t&= 19.\n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b662a09b-80ce-5d1d-8950-1d407892ead3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 736 :tutorial/title "Right Riemann Sums with Irregular Step Size"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107169 :tutorial/content "Whenever we have a fraction that contains a square root in the denominator, such as $\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},$ we often want to find an equivalent fraction that has no square root in the denominator.\n\nTo find this equivalent fraction, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the square root in the denominator. This process is known as **rationalizing the denominator**.\n\nFor example, for the fraction $\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},$ we can remove the square root from the denominator by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when we rationalize the denominator in the fraction  $\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},$ we get the equivalent fraction $\\dfrac{3 \\sqrt{2} }{ 2 }$ that has no square root in the denominator." :tutorial/id #uuid "a73f63d7-83ef-5e72-a786-df2bbc85ddd0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9068 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111823 :tutorial/content "The **power of quotient rule** states that if we want to raise the quotient $\\dfrac{x}{y}$ to some power $n,$ we must raise the numerator and denominator to the power $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^n=\\dfrac{x^n}{y^n}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{x^4}{y^4}.\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y} & = \\\\ \\frac{x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y\\cdot y\\cdot y} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." :tutorial/id #uuid "9aedc7af-544c-5314-a986-2cd6ce64479b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1484 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111824 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might need to use more than one rule of exponents to simplify an expression. First, let's remind ourselves of all of the rules of exponents.\n\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n\n- The power rule: $\\left( a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nAs an example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(u^2 v\\right)^3\n$$\n\nWe start by using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}^{2}v)}^{3}={({u}^{2})}^{3}\\cdot {v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}^{2})}^{3}\\cdot {v}^{3} & = \\\\ {u}^{(2\\cdot 3)}\\cdot {v}^{3} & = \\\\ {u}^{6}\\cdot {v}^{3} & = \\\\ {u}^{6}{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "13b1c64a-c868-5824-919e-c3a665bb4a96" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1485 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111872 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $P$ is a point in the first quadrant that lies on the unit circle. We can then draw a right triangle, where the hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point. \n\n![](images/4b/4bae3a49b5557df9e5b50280d744aa72048b2d224df042e103a6b5d156467de7.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle, its length must be equal to $1.$ And, since the triangle is a right triangle, the $(x,y)$ coordinates of any point on the unit circle in the first quadrant must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this to find the $y$-coordinate of a point given the $x$-coordinate, and vice-versa.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of a point $P$ in the first quadrant on the unit circle is $\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{3}{7}$ into the above and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t          \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty&= \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}}\\[5pt]\n\t        y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n\n}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive. So we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $y=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4029eb2-0222-5d8b-a285-7f0089655736" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2718 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111904 :tutorial/content "To simplify the expression $x\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{x^2},$ we first rewrite the radical as a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\nx \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{x^2} = x \\cdot \\left( x^2 \\right)^{1/3}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\nx \\cdot \\left( x^2 \\right)^{1/3} = x \\cdot x^{2(1/3)} = x \\cdot x^{2/3}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {x}^{2/3} & = \\\\ {x}^{1+2/3} & = \\\\ {x}^{5/3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we express the result as a radical:\n\n$$\nx^{5/3} = \\sqrt[3]{x^5}\n$$\n\nNotice that, by combining the radicals as fractional exponents and power rules, we get the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "91debf57-af23-518b-b432-ea04beccc17a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6221 :tutorial/title "Rules of Exponents and Radicals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116464 :tutorial/content "We can solve an equation involving secant over any desired domain. For example, suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sec x = 2, \\qquad -2\\pi < x \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe can proceed as usual. Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${\\color{red}x_1}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ It is in the first quadrant and has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](images/31/318eba038feb091d6e3adb80ddf325de6039bb331a7713cf1e2c02171a6388d4.png)\n\nSince the second solution $\\color{blue}x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x_2} = 2\\pi - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nHowever, we're not done yet. The domain $-2\\pi < x \\leq 2\\pi$ is larger than one period, so there are more than two solutions.\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the desired domain, we need to find angles that are coterminal with ${\\color{red}x_1}$ or ${\\color{blue}x_2}.$ So, we subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all possible solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nx_3 & = {\\color{red}x_1} -2\\pi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}- 2\\pi= -\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3},\\\nx_4 &= {\\color{blue}x_2} -2\\pi = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}- 2\\pi= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.}\n$$\n\nAdding or subtracting any more multiples of $2\\pi$ would result in solutions outside the desired domain. So, we have found all the solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are  $x = \\pm \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\pm \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b5492203-ceec-581f-aede-beedabd8c432" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2565 :tutorial/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}]
